# Everything text is LF in the working tree, not just in the index. `core.autocrlf=true` on a
# Windows checkout already stores LF, so this changes no committed content -- it stops the
# working copy from drifting to CRLF and reporting "LF will be replaced by CRLF" on every diff.
# The rules below are more specific and still win.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must check out with LF even on Windows. A CRLF shebang line fails on Linux with
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M", which names neither line endings nor the real problem.
*.sh text eol=lf

# SQL is read by DuckDB on both platforms; keep it stable so a checkout does not show as dirty.
*.sql text eol=lf

# The Windows build script is the one file that genuinely wants CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Conformance fixtures are binary and must never be line-ending translated.
*.parquet binary
